            package '3 - Behaviors' {
                doc /* Source XMI: _18_5_2_3880181_1542314598483_352913_62864
                Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1600785325374_747651_62833
                Behaviors describe the functionality of a system in terms of how it is used to achieve the goals of its various users.
                The behaviors are the concepts of operations and operation concepts.
                A behavior can be specified by a Use Case description, a Use Case diagram, or an Activity diagram.
                The Behavior architecture levels are Enterprise, CubeSat, Ground System, CubeSat Subsystems, and Ground Subsystems.
                Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1638203712957_386769_56969 (Explanation)
                Behavior Architectures and Population
                UML kind: uml:Package */
                package '3.2 Behaviors - Use Cases - Activities - Population' {
                    doc /* Source XMI: _19_0_2_3880181_1594906074544_398063_62921
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1600795354811_582354_63884
                    This package provides artifacts for establishing a relationship between a requirement and a behavior that refines the requirement.
                    The behavior can be:
                    Expressed a by text-based Use Case Description.
                    Captured in a Use Case Diagram
                    Refined by an Activity Diagram.
                    A Use Case Description can be simple narrative or have a more detailed format such as presented in our 2017 IEEE Aerospace paper:
                    Use Case Name, Scope, Primary Actor, Supporting Actor
                    Stakeholder, Precondition, Trigger, Postconditions
                    At each level there are:
                    Behavior packages containing Use Case Description and their listing table
                    Use Case packages containing Use Cases Diagrams and their listing table
                    Activity packages containing Activity Diagrams and their listing table
                    At each level there are:
                    Behavior packages containing Use Case Description documents and their listing table
                    Use Case packages containing Use Cases Diagrams and their listing table
                    Activity packages containing Activity Diagrams and their listing table
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619212079915_59749_6518 (Explanation)
                    The package provides artifacts for establishing a relationship between a requirement and a behavior that refines the requirement.
                    The behavior can be:
                    Expressed a by text-baed Behavior Description.
                    Captured in a Use Case Diagram
                    Refined by an Activity Diagram.
                    A Behavior Description can be simple narrative or have a more detailed format such as presented in our 2017 IEEE Aerospace paper:
                    Use Case Name      Scope              Primary Actor      Supporting Actor
                    Stakeholder            Precondition     Trigger                Postconditions
                    At each level there are:
                    Behavior packages containing Behavior Description and their listing table
                    Use Case packages containing Use Cases Diagrams and their listing table
                    Activity packages containing Activity Diagrams and their listing table
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619212079924_263075_6529 (Explanation)
                    As presented here, the CSRM has an example of population and relationships of requirements, behaviors, use cases, and activities.
                    They are only examples.
                    Mission modelers can populate and establish relationships to best serve their missions.
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619212079930_972947_6540 (HowTo)
                    To navigate to a package:
                    Right click on package icon
                    Scroll down to Selct in Containment Tree
                    To open table:
                    Double left click on table icon
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1637272422188_240217_56124
                    Behaviors - Use Cases - Activities - Population
                    UML kind: uml:Package */
                }
                package '3.1 Architectures: Requirements Behaviors Structures' {
                    doc /* Source XMI: _19_0_2_3880181_1594907133045_27300_62939
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1600796749349_511908_63895
                    The package architecture for requirements, structures, and behaviors at the enterprise, segment, and subsystem levels.
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619212304590_762617_6911 (Explanation)
                    Package architecture for requirements, behaviors, and structures at the enterprise, segment, and subsystem levels
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1640193116297_33492_58196 (Explanation)
                    Requirements define the What.  Behaviors and Structures specify the How.
                    Structure diagrams capture the framwork that carry out the beahaviors.
                    Activity diagrams and use case diagrams are behavior diagrams.
                    UML kind: uml:Package */
                }
            }
            package '7 - Validation Verification' {
                doc /* Source XMI: _18_5_2_3880181_1555247729018_120649_65626
                Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1600787566868_172115_62952
                The verification activity element can be populated to describe the process for verifying an element, e.g. a requirement.
                For example, the process could include verification plans, procedures, execution, results, and status.
                The validation activity element can be populated to describe the process for validating an element, e.g. a Technical Measure or a Mission Objective
                UML kind: uml:Package */
                package '7.1 - Validation Activities' {
                    doc /* Source XMI: _18_5_2_3880181_1555247792497_543158_65627
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1601051216610_397944_63169
                    Repository for validation activities.
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619539285990_672576_26539 (HowTo)
                    Instructions for adding a new Validation Activity
                    Double-click on  Activities table and then click on Add New to add a new Activity to the containment tree and to the table.
                    Right click the new element in the table then left click Select in Containment Tree to navigate to the new element.
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619539286004_522388_26561 (Explanation)
                    Validation confirms, by providing objective evidence, that the system, as-built (or as it will be built), satisfies the stakeholders’ needs, objectives, and moes. That is, the right system has been (or will be) built.
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1638981012565_865887_56678 (HowTo)
                    Instructions for establishing a relationship between a Technical Measure and a Validation Activity
                    Open the Validation Activities Table
                    Add a new Validation Activity or locate an existing one
                    Double click in the Verifies cell of the Verification Activity element to reveal three ellipses.
                    Click on the ellipses.  Search for the applicable Technical Measure.
                    Click on the Plus Sign to move the Technical Measure from the List Tab to the Selected Elements column.
                    Click on OK to populate the Verifies cell of the Verification Activity element.
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1638981700971_459219_56711 (Explanation)
                    Validation Activites - Population
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1638999927248_193119_56655 (Problem)
                    Add New does not work
                    UML kind: uml:Package */
                    action def 'Validation Activity Example' {
                        @OfficialCSRMProfile::'Validation and Verification Profile'::'ValidationActivity';
                        doc /* Source XMI: _19_0_3_2b0010b_1613264261897_122790_61460
                        Stereotype ValidationActivity
                        UML kind: uml:Activity */
                        out item parameter_0 {
                            doc /* Source XMI: _19_0_3_2b0010b_1613264348176_255610_61463
                            UML parameter name: verdict
                            UML direction: return
                            UML type: http://www.omg.org/spec/SysML/20181001/SysML.xmi#SysML_dataType.VerdictKind */
                        }
                        action node_1 {
                            doc /* Source XMI: _19_0_3_2b0010b_1613264348217_136727_61465
                            UML node kind: uml:ActivityParameterNode
                            UML node name: verdict
                            UML behavior: (none)
                            UML parameter: _19_0_3_2b0010b_1613264348176_255610_61463 */
                        }
                    }
                }
                package '7.2 - Verification Activities' {
                    doc /* Source XMI: _18_5_2_3880181_1555247819176_491088_65628
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1601051105797_584049_63168
                    Repository for verification activities.
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619539335247_87589_26602 (HowTo)
                    Instructions for adding a new Verification Activity
                    Double-click on the Activities table and then click on Add New to add a new Activity to the containment tree and to the table.
                    Right click the new element in the table then left click Select in Containment Tree to navigate to the new element.
                    Comment _19_0_3_2b0010b_1619539335256_303293_26613 (Explanation)
                    Verification confirms, by providing objective evidence, that the system and all its elements perform their intended functions and satisfy the requirements allocated to them. That is, the system has been built right.
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1638982401847_684185_57083 (HowTo)
                    Instructions for establishing a relationship between a Requirement and a Validation Activity
                    Open Validation Activities Table below.
                    Add a new Validation Activity or locate an existing one.
                    Either
                    Double click in the Verified By cell of the Technical Measure element to reveal three ellipses.
                    Click on the ellipses.  Search for the applicable Validation Activity.
                    Click on the Plus Sign to move the Validation Activity from the List Tab to the Selected Elements column.
                    Click on OK to populate the Verified By cell of the Technical Measure element
                    Note that the Traced From cell was automatically populated.
                    Double click in the Verifies cell of the Verification Activity element to reveal three ellipses.
                    Click on the ellipses.  Search for the applicable Requirement
                    .
                    Click on the Plus Sign to move the Requirement from the List Tab to the Selected Elements column
                    .
                    Click on OK to populate the Verifies cell of the Verification Activity element.
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1638985977495_454957_57259
                    Verification Activities - Population
                    Comment _19_0_2_3880181_1639001022428_934776_56913 (Problem)
                    Add New does not work properly
                    UML kind: uml:Package */
